#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375624 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$ by ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} & =\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\right)}^{2}+1 & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {\\operatorname{tan}}^{2}t+1 & ={\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375804 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9db14c13d03cb99846a40622a9633f229e8bd18942f2d03635961529e6970771.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375881 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}),$ if $2\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((-3\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c})=10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((-3\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =10 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =10 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376124 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ {v}^{2}+w \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\frac{1}{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2}+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\begin{vmatrix}2v & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w) & =0 \\\\ w-2{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w-2{v}^{2}=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376131 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=0\\,s$ and $t=1\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)=2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=0$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(0)=6(0)^{2}+2(0)=0\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(1)=6(1)^{2}+2(1)=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {8}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =128-0 \\\\  & =128\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377040 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2t+5,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2t+5\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{x-5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{x-5}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(\\frac{x-5}{2}-2)(\\frac{x-5}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x-5-4}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x-5+2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x-9}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x-3}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377076 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,2,1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,4,5⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 4)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 1)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(2\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0(-12)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(7)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(8) \\\\  & =0-14+8 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377079 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b})$, if $-3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((-2\\mathbf{b})\\times (7\\mathbf{c}))=168.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((-2\\mathbf{b})\\times (7\\mathbf{c})) & =168 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 7\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =168 \\\\ 42[\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =168 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\hspace{0.278em}=\\hspace{0.278em}-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377082 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377085 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=(t+5)(t-2),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3t-1\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{x+1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{x+1}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(\\frac{x+1}{3}+5)(\\frac{x+1}{3}-2) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x+1+15}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x+1-6}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x+16}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x-5}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377088 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =2{\\left(-\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-1 \\\\ y & =3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377091 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t=1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t & =1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t>0$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, solving for $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\sqrt{\\,1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\,}.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y=2\\sqrt{\\,1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\,}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377695 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377708 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=2x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378038 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ where $x=t{e}^{s}$ and $y=t{e}^{-s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2(t{e}^{s})}{(t{e}^{s})^{2}+(t{e}^{-s})^{2}}=\\frac{2{e}^{s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{2(t{e}^{-s})}{(t{e}^{s})^{2}+(t{e}^{-s})^{2}}=\\frac{2{e}^{-s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & ={e}^{-s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}\\cdot {e}^{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}\\cdot {e}^{-s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378552 :question/problem "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(1,0)$ is the $\\frac{1}{2}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(1,0)$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378559 :question/problem "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(3,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,-1) & =\\frac{3}{2(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}+1} & =1 \\\\ x & =2{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the parabola $x=2{y}^{2}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378566 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨8,4,6⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨8,-4,5⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨0,2,k⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 5)-4(8\\cdot k-0\\cdot 5)+6(8\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =-32k-80-32k+96 \\\\  & =16-64k \\\\  & =16(1-4k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 16(1-4k) & =0 \\\\ 1-4k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378573 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(3,0,1),$ $B(0,-1,3),$ $C(1,-2,2),$ and $D(2,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 1)+2((-2)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =6+1-12 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-5|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378622 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,\\pi )$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}y & =2\\operatorname{cos}\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378629 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}-0(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378636 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378746 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\ny+{x}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,y\\ge -{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}y\\ge -{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve $y=-{x}^{2}$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/0b/0b7a6dbbab890b469cf389fa27627e9c1a5f95d8892507fcd4ba5f70541936ea.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378773 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x+y}{(x+y)^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^{2}\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,x+y\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}x+y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378780 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3y}{\\sqrt{x+2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx+2>0\\,⟹\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}x>-2\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378787 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{y+2}}{2{x}^{3}-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}y+2\\ge 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-y\\ne 0\\end{cases}\\,⟹\\,\\begin{cases}y\\ge -2 \\\\ y\\ne 2{x}^{3}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -2,\\,y\\ne 2{x}^{3}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378794 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{-{x}^{3}y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- $-{x}^{3}y$ is defined for all $x$ and $y,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{g(x,y)}$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, ${e}^{-{x}^{3}y}$ is defined for all values of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the domain of $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378801 :question/problem "Evaluate $z=\\sqrt{x{y}^{2}-4}$ at the point $(5,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(5,-2),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=-2$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(5,-2)} & =\\sqrt{x{y}^{2}-4}\\,{|}_{(5,-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5(-2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5(4)-4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20-4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378808 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function  $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡({x}^{2}+y).$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arcsine has values between $-1$ and $1.$\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}-1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378815 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function  $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x+\\sqrt{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arccosine has values between $-1$ and $1,$ and\n\n- the expression under the square root must be non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}-1\\le x+\\sqrt{y}\\le1 \\\\ y\\ge 0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le x+\\sqrt{y}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378822 :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},1) & =\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\,{|}_{(1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6e}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378829 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+y\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,y\\ne -{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}y\\ne -{x}^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ and shade *above* and *below* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/6c/6c85783dc6626a3fea9d06431ee31e202da8c4d88e2eb1c2b6608dccdc2b782e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379335 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|=8$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379342 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+x\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=3\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 6+x\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =15-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 15-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379355 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨{k}^{2},-2,5⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨1,3,4⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨0,-1,2⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n**In ascending order:** $\\,$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}\n$\\,$\nand\n$\\,$\n$k=${{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{k}^{2} & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-1))+2(1\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 0)+5(1\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(6+4)+2(2)+5(-1) \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}+4-5 \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if \n$k=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}$ or $k=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379360 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}\\hspace{0.167em}|=12$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{m}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{m}$ onto $\\mathbf{n}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =12cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379363 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-3,2,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{s}=⟨1,2,-1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{r}$ onto $\\mathbf{s}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{s}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{s}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)(1)+(2)(2)+(4)(-1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+4-4}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379366 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨3,1,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{u}$ onto $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{u}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{v}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}_{x}{v}_{x}+{u}_{y}{v}_{y}+{u}_{z}{v}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(1)+(1)(1)+(-2)(1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+1-2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379369 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot x \\\\  & =3x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379766 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=4x-3y-1$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3y-1 & =c \\\\ 4x-3y-(c+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{c+1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{4}{3}x,$ which is a line.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{1}{3},$ which is a line different to the axes $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{4}{3}x-1,$ which is not a parabola.\n\nSo, only statement “$c=-1$ gives a line” is true."}]}
